    "C33","C40","C43","C45","C52"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Transferable L1 gold-context records in fasted/non-cirrhotic oral settings report absolute F values well above 20%: melperone 54–67%, vernakalant 58–71%, suvorexant 82%, propylthiouracil 77%, BI 425809 71.6%, levofloxacin ~100%, and hyperforin reported as high; pentazocine in cirrhotic patients also showed F ~0.68–0.70.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C05","C14","C08","C11","C13","C02","C06","C07","C12","C03","C09"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 records show high intestinal absorption or permeability for structurally similar lipophilic, chloro-substituted, basic/thiourea-containing molecules: desloratadine F_abs >90%, domperidone virtually complete absorption, UC-781 BCS II high permeability, lorcaserin high permeability and not a P-gp substrate, loratadine BCS II high permeability, indapamide 100% absorption, and difenoconazole high Papp.",
      "card_ids": ["C33","C40","C43","C35","C41","C42","C44","C38"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall/intestinal records show high bidirectional permeability and minimal or no efflux for lipophilic, chloro-substituted, basic/heteroaromatic reference molecules (loratadine Papp ~24–39.6×10^-6 cm/s, efflux ratio ≈1; domperidone Papp >1×10^-5 cm/s; lumefantrine high permeability; AFN-1252 well-absorbed with insignificant efflux; TDI-11055 moderate/high permeability with minimal efflux; decursin almost completely absorbed), reducing concern that gut-wall efflux limits the query's absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C45","C46","C50","C51","C52","C54","C56"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "High logP/logD and BCS II-like reference records indicate a possible solubility limitation, but no transferable record provides a low absolute F for the query; solubility limitation alone does not affirm F <20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C43","C42","C36"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No direct query-specific absolute F or matched oral-IV exposure is available; L3 Cmax and L4/L5 permeability/absorption evidence support the absorption component but do not quantify systemic F, so confidence remains moderate.",
      "card_ids": ["C21","C25","C33","C40","C43","C45","C52"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C45","C46","C47","C48","C49","C50","C51","C52","C53","C54","C55","C56"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct query-specific absolute oral bioavailability or matched oral-versus-IV exposure measurement is available.",
    "No query-specific aqueous solubility or dissolution data; high logP/logD and BCS II-like reference records indicate a possible solubility limitation.",
    "No query-specific first-pass hepatic metabolism or intestinal metabolism data; thiourea and prenyl/alkene groups are potential metabolic liabilities.",
    "L4/L5 records are for reference molecules, not the query; transfer is qualitative and assay/formulation context differs.",
    "No affirmative low-F evidence from a transferable context is present."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F >= 20%) with moderate confidence. L1 gold-context records uniformly report F >20% in fasted/non-cirrhotic oral settings, including structurally similar prenyl-amine and thiourea compounds (pentazocine, propylthiouracil). L4 and L5 add human absorption/Caco-2 permeability and no-efflux evidence for lipophilic chloro/basic/heteroaromatic reference molecules, supporting the absorption component and reducing gut-wall efflux concern. Solubility and first-pass metabolism remain unquantified, but no transferable low-F evidence is present; therefore the high prediction is strengthened."
}

